<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/expert/computational-neuroscience">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Computational neuroscience treats the nervous system as an <strong>information-processing
    system whose algorithms can be stated and tested</strong>. It asks what problems the brain
    solves (vision, motor control, prediction), what representations it uses (rates, spikes,
    populations), and what circuits compute them — the field where neuroscience, mathematics and
    the AI ladder of this atlas meet.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the modelling stack, from encoding to efficiency" widget="equationStep" config={compneuroCfg} />

  <h2>Foundations worth knowing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hodgkin–Huxley (1952):</strong> the first great computational model — four equations reproducing the action potential. Mechanism, not metaphor.</li>
    <li><strong>Hubel & Wiesel (1962):</strong> a computational account of visual hierarchy — simple cells build complex cells. Nobel 1981; deep networks later rediscovered the architecture.</li>
    <li><strong>Bayesian brain hypothesis:</strong> perception as statistical inference — the brain combines prior expectations with sensory evidence optimally (Knill & Pouget). Explains illusions as correct Bayesian answers to ambiguous inputs.</li>
    <li><strong>Free-energy / predictive processing:</strong> a unifying framework (Friston) — the brain minimises prediction error hierarchically. Influential, mathematically dense, and controversial in scope.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Dayan & Abbott, Theoretical Neuroscience; Knill & Pouget, TRENDS (2004); Friston (2010) free-energy principle; Yamins & DiCarlo (2016).</p>
</TopicPage>
